# DANMACHI: RETELLING OF THE LEGEND

# Chapter Seventy-One — The Loki Familia

### I. The Reports
*Finn's POV*

The Loki Familia headquarters had always possessed a certain charm.

It wasn't particularly peaceful, nor was it especially organized. There were too many adventurers coming and going, too many people arguing over training schedules, and certainly too many opportunities for Loki to cause trouble.

Nevertheless, Finn found the place comfortable.

Particularly his chair.

It was a fine piece of furniture, crafted from quality wood with a surprisingly comfortable backrest. Whoever had commissioned it had understood the importance of proper woodworking.

Finn appreciated that.

He appreciated it considerably more than the thick stack of documents currently occupying his desk.

The captain sat quietly, his fingers interlocked beneath his chin as he examined the reports.

One page.

Then another.

And another.

Every document seemed to concern the same individual.

Bell Cranel.

The white-haired adventurer who had once stood before the Loki Familia and politely declined their invitation.

A boy who had insisted on finding his own path.

A boy who, apparently, had become extraordinarily busy.

Finn slowly lowered the document.

"...What?"

He picked up another.

Then another.

His expression remained relatively composed, although the slight movement of his eyebrows suggested otherwise.

Finally, he leaned back.

"Interesting."

He looked toward the ceiling.

"Very interesting."

The door opened.

"Oi, Finn!"

Loki entered the room with her usual carefree expression. She had apparently finished whatever business she had been attending to and was now looking for something to occupy her afternoon.

Her eyes immediately settled on the papers.

"What's with all the paperwork? You look like you've just discovered the meaning of taxes."

Finn didn't answer immediately.

Instead, he tapped the documents against the desk, aligning their edges.

"Loki."

"Hm?"

"How much do you know about Bell Cranel?"

Loki blinked.

Then she grinned.

"That depends. Which Bell Cranel are we talking about? The one who rejected us, the one who keeps getting involved in ridiculous situations, or the one who's apparently collecting achievements like they're souvenirs?"

Finn slowly raised his head.

"...So you've heard."

"Heard? Finn, I was there."

"Where?"

"The Denatus."

Loki pulled a chair toward herself and sat down.

"Remember? The little gathering where the gods discuss their children, exchange information, argue about rankings, and occasionally embarrass themselves in front of everyone?"

Finn stared at her.

"I know what a Denatus is."

"Then why ask?"

"Because I'd like to know precisely how much you know."

Loki rested her elbows on the table.

"Quite a lot, actually. I was one of the judges processing the information, remember? I wasn't sitting in some tavern listening to rumors. I heard the reports, examined the presented evidence, and participated in the discussions."

She leaned forward.

"So, what's bothering you?"

Finn picked up the first report.

"Let's start with this."

He read its contents again.

"Apparently, Bell Cranel defeated an ancient monster that had been empowered through a goddess held hostage."

Loki nodded.

"Correct."

Finn turned the page.

"He subsequently defeated the Apollo Familia in a War Game after they attacked his Familia."

"Also correct."

Another page.

"And this one concerns a veteran Level Five female assassin."

Loki nodded again.

Finn slowly placed the papers on the desk.

He remained silent for several seconds.

Then—

"What the hell?"

Loki burst into laughter.

"HAHAHAHA! What's wrong? Did the reports suddenly become difficult to read?"

Finn rubbed his forehead.

"No, they're perfectly readable. That's precisely the problem."

He picked up the first document again.

"An ancient monster. A goddess held hostage. A War Game against an established Familia. An assassin who was apparently experienced enough to warrant an entire report."

He looked at Loki.

"How many incidents involving this boy are we supposed to process?"

"Well, I haven't counted."

"That's reassuring."

"You're welcome."

Finn sighed.

He had spent two months away from Orario, leading the Loki Familia's expedition through the Dungeon. Their attention had been directed toward exploration, survival, and the objectives of their expedition.

There had been little opportunity to follow the city's affairs.

And apparently, the city had not been particularly interested in remaining quiet in their absence.

"Two months," Finn muttered.

Loki tilted her head.

"What?"

"Nothing. I'm simply beginning to understand how much trouble can accumulate when I'm not around."

"Oi, don't look at me like that! I wasn't responsible for everything that happened!"

Finn gave her a rather unconvincing look.

Loki immediately raised both hands.

"Seriously! I only know what was discussed during the Denatus and what information was made available afterward. I wasn't personally involved in those incidents."

"That's not what I meant."

"Then what?"

Finn sighed again.

"Never mind."

He placed the reports together and examined the final document.

There was another matter.

One that hadn't been included in the official reports.

Something he had learned from the returning members of his Familia.

And, judging by the circumstances, something that would probably become a headache.

"Loki."

"Hm?"

"There's something else."

"Oh?"

Finn slowly stood.

He walked around the desk, his expression becoming unusually serious.

"Please don't freak out."

Loki stared at him.

"...Why would you say that?"

"Because I'd like to finish explaining before you start shouting."

"That's not particularly reassuring, you know."

Finn took a breath.

"Bell Cranel defeated Ais in a duel."

Silence.

Loki blinked.

Once.

Twice.

Then—

"...Huh?"

Finn nodded.

"Yes."

"Wait."

Loki raised a hand.

"Say that again."

"Bell Cranel defeated Ais in a duel."

"OUR Ais?"

"Unless you've acquired another Sword Princess without informing me, yes."

Loki slowly leaned backward.

Her expression became increasingly complicated.

"Now that's interesting."

Finn continued.

"Apparently, Ais used Ariel during the confrontation. However, something happened to her wind. It transformed into a black variation, and the situation became considerably more dangerous."

Loki's smile faded slightly.

"Black wind?"

"That's what the reports and accounts suggest."

Finn folded his arms.

"What's particularly interesting is that Bell didn't simply overpower her. He defeated her through a sword style remarkably similar to her own, although apparently more refined in its execution."

Loki's eyebrows rose.

"Hold on. You're telling me that Bell fought Ais using her own swordsmanship?"

"More or less."

"That's ridiculous."

"I agree."

"And Ais was the one who challenged him?"

"Yes."

Loki stared at Finn.

Then she slowly covered her mouth.

"...Pfft."

Finn narrowed his eyes.

"What?"

"Nothing."

"Loki."

"Nothing! I'm just imagining Ais asking someone for a duel and then losing to him."

She began laughing.

"HAHAHAHA! Oh, that's wonderful!"

Finn shook his head.

"You're enjoying this far too much."

"Of course I am! You should've seen your face when you read those reports!"

Finn returned to his chair.

"Unfortunately, there's another development."

Loki stopped laughing.

"Another one?"

Finn nodded.

"Our resident elf."

"...Lefiya?"

"Yes."

He placed his hands on the desk.

"Apparently, Lefiya has developed feelings for Bell."

Loki stared at him.

For several seconds, she said nothing.

Then her face slowly brightened.

"OOOOOH?"

Finn closed his eyes.

"Please don't."

"WAIT, WAIT, WAIT!"

Loki slammed both hands against the desk.

"LEFIYA?! OUR LEFIYA?!"

"Yes."

"THE SAME LEFIYA WHO GETS FLUSTERED WHEN SOMEONE COMPLIMENTS HER MAGIC?!"

"Yes."

"THE ONE WHO CAN'T EVEN HOLD A PROPER CONVERSATION WHEN SHE'S EMBARRASSED?!"

"Yes, Loki."

Loki's mouth opened.

Then she burst into laughter.

"HAHAHAHAHAHA!"

Finn massaged his temples.

"Honestly, I'm more surprised about Lefiya having a crush on Bell than Bell defeating Ais."

"That's because you're finally beginning to appreciate the important things in life!"

"What important things?"

"Romance, Finn! Romance!"

He looked at her.

"...We're discussing an adventurer who has accumulated enough incidents to fill an entire archive."

"And I'm discussing a young elf falling in love!"

"Those are not mutually exclusive."

Loki grinned.

"Exactly!"

Finn shook his head.

There was something strangely amusing about the entire situation.

Bell had once declined the Loki Familia because he wanted to pursue his own path.

Now, somehow, he had managed to become involved with several of their members without ever actually joining them.

Ais had challenged him.

Lefiya apparently admired him.

And Loki herself had developed a growing interest in the boy's activities.

It was almost impressive.

"Well," Finn eventually said, "at least nobody seems to be on particularly bad terms with the Hestia Familia."

"That's good, isn't it?"

"Very."

He glanced toward the documents.

"I'd rather not create unnecessary friction with another Familia. Especially one that appears to be attracting this much attention."

Loki nodded.

"Agreed."

Then her expression changed.

A mischievous smile slowly appeared.

"Although..."

Finn immediately looked at her.

"No."

"I haven't even said anything!"

"I know that expression."

"You're no fun."

"Thank you."

Loki leaned back in her chair.

"Still, I wonder what Bell would say if he found out that our little Lefiya has a crush on him."

Finn returned his attention to the reports.

"I suspect that would depend on whether he's already noticed."

Loki's grin widened.

"Oh, this is going to be entertaining."

Finn sighed.

He was beginning to regret asking.

