### II. An Uncomfortable Recovery
*Ais's POV*

The Twilight Manor was considerably louder than usual.

The twins were arguing.

Again.

"Oi, Tione! I said I was going first!"

"And I said you could wait, Tiona!"

"Why should I wait?!"

"Because I said so!"

"That's not a reason!"

Nearby, Bete was expressing his dissatisfaction with the latest group of recruits.

"Are you idiots trying to get yourselves killed?!"

A young adventurer stumbled backward after narrowly avoiding a strike.

Bete clicked his tongue.

"Your footing is terrible! If you move like that in the Dungeon, you'll be dead before you even realize what happened!"

He pointed toward another recruit.

"And you! Stop staring at your weapon! Watch the monster!"

The training continued.

However, Ais wasn't participating.

She had already excused herself.

Her body wasn't particularly injured, but she needed some rest.

More importantly, she needed time to think.

She sat on the edge of her bed, looking down at her hands.

They were perfectly ordinary.

Familiar.

Yet the memories of the duel remained unusually vivid.

Bell.

His movements.

His swordsmanship.

The way he had understood her technique.

And that black wind.

Ais slowly closed her fingers.

"How would he react?"

The question escaped her lips before she could stop herself.

She lowered her head.

"If I told him..."

She hesitated.

"...that I'm not human."

Her expression became troubled.

Would he be disgusted?

Would he look at her differently?

Perhaps he would even become frightened.

Ais had never particularly cared about how other people perceived her.

But Bell was different.

She didn't want him to look at her with fear.

She didn't want him to avoid her.

And she certainly didn't want to lose the strange familiarity she had begun to associate with him.

"No."

She shook her head.

"I think he already knows."

Ais remembered the way Bell had looked at her.

He had recognized something.

Perhaps more than she had initially realized.

Then another thought occurred to her.

"He knows my father."

Her eyes widened.

"...Wait."

She stared at the floor.

"My father died a thousand years ago."

A thousand years.

The number was almost absurd when spoken aloud.

Bell couldn't possibly be that old.

Could he?

She began reconstructing the conversation.

His knowledge.

His familiarity with her father.

The unusual things he had said.

Then, gradually, an explanation formed in her mind.

"Oh."

She nodded to herself.

"So that's it."

Ais looked toward the window.

"I'm a demi-spirit."

She paused.

"That means he must be a demi-spirit too."

She seemed rather pleased with her conclusion.

Unfortunately, her reasoning was entirely incorrect.

Bell's nature was considerably more complicated than the explanation she had just constructed.

But Ais had reached her conclusion, and for the moment, it was enough.

She slowly lay down.

"...I'll ask him later."

Her eyes closed.

Within minutes, she had fallen asleep.

