### III. An Unwelcome Visitor
*Loki's POV*

The afternoon was pleasant.

The streets of Orario were lively, merchants were calling out their wares, and adventurers were moving between the various establishments scattered throughout the city.

Loki walked with her hands behind her head.

She needed some fresh air.

Too much paperwork was bad for her health.

Especially when Finn kept bringing it up.

"Honestly, that little potato-headed captain needs to learn how to relax."

She chuckled.

Then she noticed someone approaching.

A familiar face.

A certain god with purple hair and an expression that always seemed a little too theatrical.

Dionysus.

Beside him walked his Level Four captain, Filvis Chalia.

Loki slowed.

Dionysus noticed her and raised a hand.

"Loki! Long time no see."

He approached with an amiable smile.

Loki looked at him.

Then at Filvis.

Then back at him.

"What do you want?"

Dionysus blinked.

"My, my. Is that how you greet an old acquaintance?"

"Don't give me that. I know you, Dionysus. You don't approach someone without a reason, and your face is practically advertising that you're in need of something."

Dionysus placed a hand against his chest.

"How cruel. I merely wished to exchange a few words."

"Then exchange them."

"Ah, but..."

He glanced toward the surrounding streets.

"I would prefer somewhere a little more private."

Loki narrowed her eyes.

Then she shrugged.

"Fine. You're welcome to come to my manor."

"How generous."

"Don't mistake hospitality for trust."

Dionysus laughed.

Filvis remained silent.

Loki turned and began walking.

The two followed.

---

The conversation took place inside one of the manor's reception rooms.

Loki sat comfortably on the sofa, while Dionysus took the seat opposite her. Filvis remained standing beside her patron.

A servant brought refreshments.

Dionysus thanked them politely.

Then he finally addressed the reason for his visit.

"Loki, I've heard that your Familia encountered some rather unusual monsters during your recent Dungeon activities."

Loki raised an eyebrow.

"Have you?"

"Indeed. Strange, worm-like creatures. My own children encountered similar specimens."

His expression gradually became somber.

"Unfortunately, several of them died during that encounter."

Loki looked at him.

Dionysus lowered his gaze.

A faint redness appeared around his eyes.

"Such a terrible loss. Adventurers who had families, friends, and futures ahead of them..."

He paused.

"Well, I'm sure you understand."

Loki didn't answer immediately.

She watched him carefully.

Dionysus continued.

"I was hoping we might cooperate in investigating the matter. My Familia has already made inquiries with the Guild, but they've been rather uncooperative."

He sighed dramatically.

"They're keeping information from us. Information that might help prevent further casualties."

Loki tilted her head.

"Is that so?"

"Unfortunately."

"Interesting."

Dionysus nodded.

"I was hoping your Familia might be able to provide some assistance. Given your experience with the deeper floors, I'm certain you have access to information that could help us understand these creatures."

Loki slowly leaned forward.

"Dionysus."

"Yes?"

"You're asking for information concerning the deeper floors?"

"Precisely."

"From my Familia?"

"Well, considering your Familia's achievements, I thought it would be reasonable to—"

"That's impossible."

Dionysus stopped.

Loki crossed her arms.

"You're a little confused about how the Guild handles that sort of information, aren't you?"

She smiled, although there was little warmth in it.

"Access to deeper-floor information isn't handed out simply because someone asks nicely. Your Familia's rank limits the information available to you. There are restrictions, classifications, and requirements."

Dionysus's expression remained pleasant.

Loki continued.

"You can't expect the Guild to hand over everything just because you're curious. If your Familia wants access to information reserved for higher-ranked organizations, then you'll have to earn that privilege."

"Of course. I understand that."

"Do you?"

Loki tilted her head.

"Then why are you blaming the Guild for refusing to provide something your Familia isn't entitled to receive?"

Dionysus fell silent.

Filvis lowered her gaze.

Loki took a sip of her drink.

"Look, I'm not saying the situation isn't concerning. If these creatures are appearing on multiple floors, then it's worth investigating. But you can't just accuse the Guild of hiding information because you didn't get the answers you wanted."

Dionysus smiled weakly.

"You're quite right."

"Besides, if I happen to acquire anything relevant, I'll have you informed."

His expression brightened.

"Really?"

"Of course."

Loki shrugged.

"But don't expect me to go digging through every restricted report just because you've asked."

Dionysus placed a hand against his chest.

"That is more than enough. Thank you, Loki."

He stood.

Then bowed politely.

"I appreciate your consideration."

Loki waved her hand.

"Yeah, yeah. Don't mention it."

Dionysus turned toward the door.

Filvis followed.

"Thank you for your hospitality."

Loki smiled.

"Anytime."

The door closed.

And the room became quiet.

Loki remained seated.

Her expression gradually lost its carefree appearance.

"...What was that?"

She looked toward the door.

Dionysus had been unusually interested in the creatures.

Not merely concerned.

Interested.

His questions had been too specific, his explanations too carefully arranged, and his request for information far too convenient.

Something wasn't right.

She tapped her fingers against the armrest.

"Strange."

She remembered the reports from the expedition.

The unusual creatures.

The deaths.

The circumstances surrounding their appearance.

And now Dionysus wanted information concerning them.

Perhaps it was simply a coincidence.

Perhaps his Familia genuinely wanted to understand what had happened.

But Loki had lived long enough to know that coincidences could sometimes be remarkably convenient.

She leaned back.

"Well, that's dangerous business you're getting yourself involved in, Dionysus."

Her eyes narrowed.

"What exactly are you planning to do with that information?"

She glanced toward the window.

The afternoon sunlight continued illuminating the city.

Orario appeared peaceful.

But Loki knew better.

There were always things happening beneath the surface.

And lately, far too many of them seemed connected.

She picked up her drink.

"Finn's going to hate hearing about this."

A small smile returned to her face.

"Especially after all that paperwork."

She laughed quietly.

"Poor little captain."

But her amusement didn't last long.

Her thoughts returned to Dionysus.

There was something about that encounter she couldn't quite shake.

Something that made her instincts uncomfortable.

And Loki had learned, over countless years, that ignoring such instincts was rarely a wise decision.

**Dionysus was hiding something.**

Of that, she was increasingly certain.

And whatever it was, she intended to find out before it became another problem for Orario.
